  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: PHP_FOREACH
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: PHP_FOREACH
  language: 2
  conceptTitle: Looping Through an Array with foreach
  symbol: foreach
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - PHP_10_MIXED
  - PHP_TYPE_10_FOREACH
  topics:
  - Mixed
  definition: 'foreach is a loop made for arrays: it runs its code once for every
    item in the array.'
  howItWorks: 'Write foreach, then in round brackets: the array, the keyword as,
    and a new variable name for the current item. Inside the curly braces, that variable
    holds one item at a time, from the first to the last.'
  exampleCode: "<?php\n$pets = [\"cat\", \"dog\", \"fish\"];\nforeach ($pets as $pet)
    {\n    echo \"I like the \" . $pet . \"\\n\";\n}\n?>"
  exampleExplanation: 'The loop runs three times. The first time $pet is cat, then
    dog, then fish, so it outputs three lines: I like the cat, I like the dog and
    I like the fish.'
  tip: Other languages use different words in their loops, so always check the PHP
    way of writing it.
